<p>I was about to try and defend my object class choice as I have a more lax/flexible definition of the classes than a lot of others, but I realised that even by my standards this leans more towards Keter than Euclid, so I've changed it.</p>
<p>The lack of response from authorities or organised response from others is intended to be covered by the line about secondary anomalous properties which suppress the response of others, as an extension of the compulsion cliche. If you think this is an unsatisfying cop-out that's entirely fair, but I did see that plot hole and try to paper it over.</p>

